\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

We identify seven limitations that bound the scope of our conclusions.

\paragraph{Scale ceiling.}
All experiments use models at or below 3B parameters. Larger models (7B+) may exhibit qualitatively different contamination dynamics where SFT injection is absorbed into the parameter space without producing measurable signal divergence. The dose-response attenuation we observe between 0.5B and 3B (Section~\ref{sec:domain_differential}) suggests that contamination effects diminish with scale, but whether they vanish entirely or plateau remains untested.

\paragraph{Signal coverage.}
S1 (embedding similarity) requires access to the training corpus, which is unavailable for closed-source models. For API-only models, only S2 (Min-K\%++) and S4 (Self-Critique) are applicable, and both show poor discrimination at scales below 1.5B (Section~\ref{sec:signal_failure}). The framework's practical applicability to closed-source evaluation settings remains unvalidated.

\paragraph{Ensemble size and per-domain power.}
Our simulation study establishes that reliable $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$ respondents. Our primary MMLU/ARC analysis uses $J = 13$ after excluding 0.5B-scale models due to position bias confounds, well below this threshold. At $J = 13$, only one of eight MMLU domains (Social Sciences) yields a $\gamma$ estimate with a credible interval excluding zero. The remaining domain estimates show directional patterns (positive STEM, negative non-STEM) but lack statistical power for individual significance. Per-domain $\gamma$ diagnosis at current ensemble sizes should be treated as exploratory rather than confirmatory.

\paragraph{Position bias as systematic confound.}
Qwen2.5-0.5B exhibits answer-position bias that correlates with contamination dosage (A-option selection rate: 46\% at d000 to 98\% at d100), necessitating exclusion from multiple-choice IRT analyses. This is not unique to our setup: position bias is a known issue across small-scale LLMs under logprob-based evaluation. The exclusion reduces ensemble size and statistical power, creating a tension between including more models (higher $J$) and ensuring that response patterns reflect genuine item-level effects rather than format artifacts.

\paragraph{Credible interval coverage.}
The 50\% posterior credible intervals achieve approximately 35\% empirical coverage, indicating that the intervals are too narrow. In practice, this means the Bayesian uncertainty quantification functions as a directional indicator (positive vs.\ negative $\gamma$, credible interval excluding zero or not) rather than a precise probability statement. The calibration guarantee is weaker than the Bayesian framework nominally promises.

\paragraph{Model misfit in non-STEM domains.}
The 2PL+$\gamma$ specification may be inadequate for domains where contamination effects are non-monotone. A single linear $\gamma$ cannot capture relationships where memorization helps on some items but hurts on others within the same domain. Richer contamination response functions or multidimensional ability specifications may be required.

\paragraph{Single injection protocol.}
We use LoRA-based SFT injection exclusively. Contamination that occurs during full-parameter fine-tuning or pretraining-stage data leakage may produce different distributional signatures that our signal battery does not capture. Our controlled injection paradigm validates the IRT framework under known conditions but does not guarantee transferability to all contamination mechanisms.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

We discuss limitations in Appendix~\ref{sec:limitations}, including scale ceiling (all models $\leq$3B), signal coverage gaps for closed-source models, ensemble size constraints ($J=13$ vs.\ the $J \geq 50$ needed for reliable $\gamma$ recovery), position bias confounds at the 0.5B scale, and under-coverage of posterior credible intervals.

\paragraph{Implications for practice.}
Our findings carry four recommendations. For benchmark developers, IRT-based calibration should be standard infrastructure: vanilla 2PL alone reduces MAE by 53\% on ARC without any detection signals. For detection practitioners, Min-K\%++ should not be trusted below 1.5B parameters due to directional reversal (\S\ref{sec:signal_failure}). For leaderboard operators, adding IRT to evaluation pipelines is a low-cost improvement that does not require contamination detection. For researchers, per-domain breakdowns should be reported alongside aggregate scores, as contamination may affect cognitive domains differently.

\paragraph{Future directions.}
Scaling to $J \geq 50$ by pooling respondents across checkpoints, seeds, and training configurations would enable confirmatory tests of the directional heterogeneity observed here. Multidimensional IRT could model domain-specific ability dimensions that interact differently with contamination. Active signal selection via Fisher information optimization could reduce the requirement for full corpus access, enabling application to closed-source models.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Contamination detection and mitigation.}
Membership inference approaches include Min-K\% \citep{shi_2023_minkprob} and Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} (token-level surprisal), ConStat \citep{dekoninck2024constat} (accuracy gaps on paraphrased items), CDD/TED \citep{dong_2024_cdd} (completion-based probes), KDS \citep{choi_2025_kds} (kernel divergence), and TS-Guessing \citep{deng_2024_tsguessing} (temporal splits). Concurrent with our work, \citet{tao2025detecting} propose an entropy collapse method for RL-trained models. These methods flag contamination but do not produce adjusted scores. On the mitigation side, \citet{sun2025emperor} systematically evaluate twenty approaches (rephrasing, option shuffling, dynamic construction) and find that none reliably restores clean performance, motivating post-hoc correction. DCR \citep{xu2025dcr} estimates decontaminated scores via fuzzy inference but produces point estimates without uncertainty quantification or domain-level diagnosis. ContamCal consumes detection signals as inputs and feeds them into a calibration framework that estimates decontaminated ability with credible intervals.

\paragraph{IRT for LLM evaluation.}
Item Response Theory has gained traction as a principled alternative to raw accuracy. \citet{lalor_2024_irt_nlp} provide a comprehensive IRT tutorial for NLP; TinyBenchmarks \citep{polo_2024_tinybenchmarks} uses IRT-informed item selection for efficient evaluation; Growing Pains \citep{habba_2025_growing_pains} validates IRT at scale on 400+ models and identifies low-respondent limitations; HiBayES \citep{luettgau_2025_hibayes} adds hierarchical Bayesian inference for credible intervals; ATLAS \citep{li_2024_atlas} applies IRT for adaptive testing. All treat items as equally trustworthy. ContamCal augments IRT with contamination covariates $\gamma_k$ within the item response function, drawing on the educational measurement tradition of item compromise modeling \citep{eckerly_2017_item_compromise,sinharay_2017_preknowledge}, DIF cancellation \citep{ercikan_2008_dif_cancellation}, and explanatory IRT \citep{deboeck_2004_explanatory_irt}, with three adaptations for LLM evaluation: continuous exposure scores, domain-level effects, and counterfactual inference (\S\ref{sec:formulation}).

\paragraph{Calibration disambiguation.}
We use ``calibration'' in the psychometric sense: adjusting scores to approximate clean (uncontaminated) performance. This differs from probability calibration \citep{guo_2017_calibration}, where the goal is aligning predicted confidences with empirical frequencies. Our reliability diagrams compare calibrated scores against held-out clean accuracy, not predicted probabilities against observed outcomes.

